### Verified result
All 43 pack sources match their frozen snapshots and current bytes. All 27
hash-bearing artifact rows match. The 28th row explicitly declares the manifest
itself with null size/hash; that is not a failed artifact. The manifest and
submission hashes independently match the return pointer. Prime's first checker
misclassified that self row; the corrected checker and qualification are retained.
Prime reran 14 tests on Windows Python 3.12.14: all passed in 41.365 seconds, no
skips. The test module was imported and unittest invoked without its __main__
writer, preserving builder transcripts. Its PY harness variable was set to the
available interpreter; product bytes were unchanged. The independent log is kept.
The 0-space loop preserves full supplied wording and outside-section content,
records then renders, survives a new-process read, keeps a review current after
mechanical rendering, and detects changed task/evidence/receipt/agreement. Held
questions, duplicate judgment, partial recovery and historical interpretations
are covered by the returned suite. These are synthetic judgments, not a fresh
Prime test. Prime did not repeat the builder's separate 32 old-cycle tests here.

### RL-01 - real producer and consumer do not connect
Prime used 0521's unmodified returned tool to create and verify a real synthetic
capture at 0199.synth-lab for 0599.synth-lab/05.01, then passed its unchanged
descriptor to prepare. The capture was complete; prepare returned exit 0 but
full_success false, pass_state conflict, no template, and reason target_page is
not the derived review destination. The producer names the return page, while
the consumer expects the review face. A diagnostic copy changing only that field
exposed the second mismatch: custody path is not a regular file. The producer
correctly supplies a custody directory. These are diagnostic descriptor edits,
not valid outputs or workarounds. Original descriptor and result are preserved.
Prime's seam omitted sufficiently precise field semantics/types, which helped
allow this divergence. Clarify that adapter explicitly; do not silently reinterpret
existing descriptors or revise the shared contract beneath other workers.

### RL-02 - hashing a descriptor's chosen files does not verify custody
observe_descriptor hashes the named receipt and return, and checks existence of
manifest, custody and verification evidence, but does not validate the actual
receipt schema/origin/submission, reservation chain, manifest links or payloads.
A diagnostic fixture with a custody placeholder file and a receipt explicitly
naming a different page/pass and invalid receipt schema still reached
observation.state complete and pass_state awaiting-review when its descriptor
hashes were updated. This did not approve work, but it falsely established a
complete input observation. Test fixtures use synthetic receipt/manifest formats,
so their passing checks do not establish compatibility with real capture records.
Repair by using a declared pinned custody reader/validator and checking actual
origin, line, pass, receipt/manifest/reservation/payload relationships, including
legacy relocation provenance. Do not trust verification.result or arbitrary
existing evidence files as proof. Unknown formats remain explicitly unsupported
or unverified and cannot become a fresh complete input.

### RL-03 - nonzero rendering remains incomplete
The builder accurately reports that pass02 can record a nonzero N1NN review but
the frozen constructor still invokes pass01 and refuses bad member on rendering.
Judgment stays render-pending. Keep that preservation behavior, but complete a
versioned constructor/pin successor before calling the declared N-space path
integrated. This pass disclosed the limit; it is not an undisclosed success claim.

